Owner ruling 2026-08-23, resolving the open question the trust store shipped
with. The previous behaviour was advisory for everything: a change was
reported, the grant stood, and the next run executed the new build script.

THE SPLIT IS THE SUBSTANCE. build.rs is the file that actually EXECUTES during
indexing and it changes rarely, so a prompt about it carries information.
Cargo.toml churns with routine dependency bumps, and re-prompting on that is
how a person learns to click through the prompt that matters — the reasoning
this project already wrote down in TestRustAnalyzerDisclosureRespectsTheGate.

MEASURED END TO END on an authored fixture (harmless build script, authored
here — arm never points at untrusted code):
  granted, unchanged        -> 12 origin=scip nodes
  Cargo.toml bumped         -> still granted, still 12, surfaced on --show
  build.rs edited           -> 0 scip nodes; the modified script never ran

REVOCATION IS APPLIED AT THE SINGLE READ POINT, not at each caller.
read_decision returns the EFFECTIVE answer, so a consumer cannot forget the
check — and forgetting would fail OPEN, executing a build script the user
never approved. recorded_grant keeps what was written down, so --show can
distinguish "you declined" from "you granted and the build script then
changed"; conflating those would misreport what happened. The gate's behaviour
is asserted through the RESOLVER it actually calls, not through the store
alone.

THE LIMIT IS STATED, and was on the table when the ruling was made: only
TOP-LEVEL manifests are hashed, so no form of this protects against a
DEPENDENCY's build script. It catches tampering with this project's own build
script and nothing else — which is part of why full strictness, at its
habituation cost, was not worth it.

THE FOUR TESTS THAT PINNED THE OLD BEHAVIOUR NAMED THEMSELVES as the ones to
change if OQ1 resolved, and were changed rather than deleted. The rewrite adds
the DISCRIMINATING case the split needs — a Cargo.toml change that must NOT
revoke. Without it, "revoke on any change" passes every other assertion and
the ruling is silently over-implemented.

